    steps: [
      { text: "Drift is any difference between real infrastructure and the code. The top row makes it; the bottom half finds it across every state, routes it and decides what to do. The controller on the right is the other model: no reports, just constant correction." },
      { text: "Many writers make drift: a console fix during an incident, a script or second IaC stack, an autoscaler that owns counts, and the cloud filling in defaults or upgrading a managed component. Some of it is expected and should never be reverted.", on: ["console", "tools", "scaler", "defaults", "live"], flow: ["e-click", "e-tools", "e-scale", "e-def"] },
      { text: "A scheduled job plans every state with `-detailed-exitcode`: 0 no changes, 1 error, 2 changes. Exit 2 also fires for merged code never applied, so a `-refresh-only` plan is the drift check: it shows only what changed outside Terraform.", on: ["plan", "live", "code", "exit"], flow: ["e-refresh", "e-read", "e-exit"] },
      { text: "Thousands of nightly plans refresh every resource, hit provider rate limits and slow real deploys. Stagger schedules, lower `-parallelism`, and keep locking on: a lock the job can't get within `-lock-timeout` is a skipped check to retry, not drift.", on: ["plan", "live"], flow: ["e-refresh"] },
      { text: "Route each finding to the team that owns the state. Workspace tags, a `CODEOWNERS` path or a catalog entry name the owner, and drift opens a ticket in their queue with the plan attached. One org-wide drift channel gets ignored.", on: ["exit", "alert", "owner"], flow: ["e-yes", "e-route"] },
      { text: "Noise kills the signal. Perpetual diffs from APIs that normalize values train people to ignore every report. Track drift per resource address over time, fix or `ignore_changes` the known perpetual diffs, and alert only on new drift.", on: ["alert", "owner"] },
      { text: "Revert: apply the code, the default for a change nobody approved. Adopt: change the code or import the resource so the next plan is clean. A `-refresh-only` apply only writes the drift into state, so the next normal apply reverts it.", on: ["owner", "revert", "adopt", "code", "live"], flow: ["e-revert", "e-apply", "e-adopt", "e-edit"] },
      { text: "Watch for silent gaps. HCP Terraform health assessments need Standard or Premium and pause when a workspace's latest run errored, so broken workspaces stop being checked. Its Explorer lists drifted workspaces. Alert on workspaces whose last assessment is old.", on: ["plan", "exit"] },
      { text: "Plans only refresh what's already in state, so hand-made resources in no state never show as drift. AWS Config records changes to supported resources, managed or not; diff that inventory against the IDs in all states.", on: ["config", "live"], flow: ["e-config"] },
      { text: "Crossplane-style controllers revert out-of-band changes on every poll. There's no drift report, but an emergency console fix is undone too unless you pause the resource first.", on: ["ctl", "live"], flow: ["e-ctl"] },
      { text: "Prevention beats detection. Make the console read-only by default, keep write access in a logged break-glass role, and back-port every break-glass change to code, or revert it, in a follow-up PR.", on: ["console", "owner", "code"] },
    ],
    say30: "Across thousands of states I treat drift as a routed signal. A scheduled `-refresh-only` plan runs for every state, staggered so it doesn't eat API quota, and a lock it can't get is a skipped check, not drift. Each finding opens a ticket for the team that owns that state, and I alert only on new drift so known perpetual diffs don't train people to ignore it. The owner decides: revert by applying the code, or adopt by changing the code or importing. I also watch for gaps: workspaces whose checks stopped, and resources in no state at all, found by diffing an inventory. Prevention is a read-only console with break-glass.",
    gotchas: [
      "`ignore_changes` hides drift on those attributes forever, including a malicious or mistaken edit. List only attributes another system owns, comment why, and avoid `all`, which stops Terraform from ever proposing updates.",
      "A continuous reconciler undoes break-glass fixes. Pause it first (`crossplane.io/paused: \"true\"` in Crossplane). Reddit stopped an autoscaler by hand and Puppet, seeing drift, turned it back on mid-migration.",
      "Exit code 2 on a normal plan is any pending change, including merged code never applied. Apply on merge so a deploy gap and real drift don't mix.",
    ],
    agentic: [
      "An agent can turn a drift ticket into a draft fix: a revert PR, or an adopt PR with code edits and `import` blocks, with the refresh-only plan attached. Revert versus adopt depends on why someone clicked, which the plan does not show, so the owning team decides; the failure modes are adopting a mistaken or malicious console change into code, or adding `ignore_changes` just to quiet the report.",
      "Drift output carries text written by whoever changed the cloud: tags, descriptions, rule names. An agent that reads it and can open PRs is acting on untrusted input, so keep it read-only plus PR-only with no secrets in reach, and let plan policy check whatever it proposes.",
      "An agent that applies its own drift fixes on a schedule becomes a Crossplane-style reconciler with no pause annotation, and will undo break-glass fixes mid-incident. Pulumi Neo shows the safer shape: it runs scheduled automations and usually hands changes off as PRs with a preview summary, so branch protection and reviewers still gate them, and `pulumi neo --permission-mode read-only` blocks state-mutating operations (its docs give no GA or preview label).",
    ],
    quiz: [
      { q: "What do the three exit codes of `terraform plan -detailed-exitcode` mean, and why is 2 not the same as drift?", a: "0: no changes. 1: error. 2: changes present. Exit 2 also happens when merged code has not been applied yet, so you need a `-refresh-only` plan (or apply-on-merge) to tell outside changes from a deploy backlog." },
      { q: "An on-call engineer opened a port in the console during an incident. The next morning the scheduled plan reports drift. What are the two ways to resolve it?", a: "Revert: apply the code and the port closes again. Adopt: add the rule to the code (and import it if it is a new resource) in a reviewed PR, so the next plan is clean. A `-refresh-only` apply alone does not adopt it." },
      { q: "How does drift handling differ between Terraform and Crossplane?", a: "Terraform only notices drift when someone runs a plan, and only changes things on apply. Crossplane controllers reconcile continuously and revert out-of-band changes on every poll, so drift is fixed automatically, including emergency fixes, unless the resource is paused or set to Observe." },
    ],
    code: [
      { lang: "shell", label: "Nightly drift check per state", src: "terraform init -input=false\nterraform plan -refresh-only -detailed-exitcode \\\n  -input=false -lock-timeout=10m -out=drift.tfplan 2> plan.err\ncase $? in\n  0) echo \"no drift\" ;;\n  2) terraform show -no-color drift.tfplan > drift.txt\n     ./open-ticket --team \"$OWNER\" drift.txt ;;  # your tooling\n  *) if grep -q \"Error acquiring the state lock\" plan.err; then\n       echo \"state busy: drift check skipped\"  # retry next run\n     else cat plan.err; echo \"plan failed\"; exit 1; fi ;;\nesac" },
      { lang: "hcl", label: "Drift you expect: let the autoscaler own it", src: "resource \"aws_ecs_service\" \"api\" {\n  name          = \"api\"\n  desired_count = 3   # initial value only\n\n  lifecycle {\n    # Application Auto Scaling changes this.\n    ignore_changes = [desired_count]\n  }\n}" },
    ],
    codeNote: "The shell job uses a refresh-only plan, so merged-but-unapplied code does not show up as drift. `ignore_changes` still uses the value on create; it only stops later updates.",
