    steps: [
      { text: "Layers from the PR to the database. The top row lives in the pipeline and depends on the code being right. The bottom row lives in the cloud and holds in every account even when the code, or a cleanup script, is wrong." },
      { text: "Split roles per state or account. The PR plan role can read but not change anything. One admin CI role for every state would turn any pipeline compromise into an org-wide one.", on: ["pr", "plan"], flow: ["e-pr"] },
      { text: "Lifecycle rules act at plan time: `prevent_destroy` fails any plan that would destroy the object, and `create_before_destroy` builds the replacement first. But they live in config. Delete the block or the module call and the rule goes too.", on: ["plan", "life"], flow: ["e-life"] },
      { text: "Reviewers miss one `-/+` in a 2,000-line plan, so make it data. In `terraform show -json`, any `change.actions` containing `delete` is a delete or replace. Count those per resource type in CI; OPA's Terraform guide scores a plan's blast radius with weights per type and action.", on: ["plan", "json", "q"], flow: ["e-show", "e-check"] },
      { text: "With no deletes or replaces, the plan goes on. Any on a stateful type needs a human who reads the `-/+` lines and the `# forces replacement` marker. Shared modules fan out, as when one hardening change rebuilt 1Password's Device Trust load balancers, so roll module versions per stack.", on: ["q", "review"], flow: ["e-flag"] },
      { text: "The apply job uses a separate role and applies the saved plan file, so what was reviewed is what runs.", on: ["q", "review", "apply"], flow: ["e-auto", "e-approve"] },
      { text: "Outside Terraform, a permissions boundary caps the apply role even if someone widens its policy, and let the apply role create only roles that carry the same boundary. SCPs deny actions for every role in member accounts, root user included.", on: ["apply", "guard"], flow: ["e-call"] },
      { text: "SCPs are org-wide blast radius too: a bad deny can lock out every account. AWS recommends testing on an OU and moving accounts in a few at a time. SCPs never apply to the management account, so keep apply roles out of it.", on: ["guard"] },
      { text: "With deletion protection on, the delete call fails until a separate, visible change turns it off. With `create_before_destroy`, the new database already exists when the old one's delete fails, so you are left with both.", on: ["guard", "api", "db"], flow: ["e-allow", "e-del"] },
      { text: "Cleanup automation deletes from stale lists: Azure OpenAI lost GPU and endpoint resources in 14 regions when a global config marked resource groups unused. Use resource locks, roll config changes region by region, and dry-run deletes with a diff someone reads.", on: ["api", "db"] },
      { text: "Last line: backups that survive the delete. Take a final snapshot, keep automated backups on deletion, and copy backups to another Region.", on: ["db", "backup"], flow: ["e-snap"] },
      { text: "`-target` plans only the named resources and their dependencies. It is for recovery, never routine: it skips the rest of the plan, so drift and pending changes go unseen.", on: ["target", "api"], flow: ["e-target"] },
    ],
    say30: "I layer it, and the layers that matter most at org scale are the ones outside the code. In the pipeline: per-state roles, a read-only plan role, plan JSON parsed in CI so any delete or replace on a stateful type needs a human, and the saved plan applied by a separate role. `prevent_destroy` lives in config, so deleting the block deletes the protection. In the cloud: permissions boundaries on apply roles, SCPs tested on one OU before the root, deletion protection, and backups that survive deletion. Cleanup automation gets dry runs and region-by-region rollout, and shared modules roll out per stack. `-target` is for recovery only.",
    gotchas: [
      "`prevent_destroy` is not stored in state. To stop managing something without deleting it, use a `removed` block with `lifecycle { destroy = false }` (Terraform 1.7+; see Terraform, Five Years On).",
      "Default replacement order is destroy, then create. crates.io lost DNS for about 10 minutes when one apply deleted records, waited for a certificate, then recreated them.",
      "`create_before_destroy` needs unique names because old and new exist together, and Terraform turns it on for the resources this one depends on too.",
      "Deletion protection and `prevent_destroy` don't stop data loss from a bad write, a dropped table, or a script calling the API. Only backups and soft deletes cover those.",
    ],
    agentic: [
      "Agent-written refactors fail the way human ones do, just more often per day: deleting a resource block or module call takes `prevent_destroy` with it. That makes the cloud-side layers (SCPs, permissions boundaries, deletion protection) matter more, because they still hold when the agent's code is wrong and the reviewer missed the `-/+`.",
      "OWASP LLM06:2025 Excessive Agency names three causes: too much functionality, permission and autonomy. An agent holding the apply role with `-target` and no prompt has all three; the fix is complete mediation, with authorization in IAM and the cloud API rather than in the agent's instructions, and Pulumi's `--approval-mode auto` (no prompts at all) kept to sandbox or read-only use.",
      "Google's AI-in-SRE paper sends agent changes through a safety gateway with mandatory dry runs, rate limits and a Red Button to pause or revoke. In IaC the saved plan is the dry run, and the red button should flip without merging code: a trust policy or SCP that cuts off every agent role at once, tested before you need it.",
    ],
    quiz: [
      { q: "A resource has `prevent_destroy = true`. A PR deletes its resource block. What happens?", a: "Terraform destroys it. The rule lives in configuration and is not recorded in state, so it disappears with the block. Use a `removed` block with `destroy = false`, or rely on deletion protection in the cloud." },
      { q: "How do you find every destructive change in a plan automatically?", a: "Save the plan, run `terraform show -json`, and look at `resource_changes[].change.actions`. Any list containing `delete` is a delete or replace (`[\"delete\",\"create\"]` or `[\"create\",\"delete\"]`). `action_reason` and `replace_paths` explain why." },
      { q: "Why split the plan role and the apply role?", a: "Plans run on every PR, including unreviewed code, so that role should only read. The apply role can change infrastructure and should only run from the protected branch on a reviewed saved plan, with a permissions boundary capping it." },
    ],
    code: [
      { lang: "hcl", label: "Belt and braces on a database", src: "resource \"aws_db_instance\" \"orders\" {\n  # ...\n  deletion_protection       = true   # provider default: false\n  skip_final_snapshot       = false\n  final_snapshot_identifier = \"orders-final\"\n  delete_automated_backups  = false  # default: true\n\n  lifecycle {\n    prevent_destroy = true\n  }\n}" },
      { lang: "shell", label: "List destructive actions in CI", src: "terraform plan -out=tfplan -input=false\nterraform show -json tfplan |\n  jq -r '.resource_changes[]\n    | select(.change.actions | index(\"delete\"))\n    | \"\\(.change.actions | join(\"/\")) \\(.address)\"'" },
    ],
    codeNote: "`prevent_destroy` fails the plan; `deletion_protection` makes the RDS API refuse the delete even if the code changes. The AWS provider defaults `delete_automated_backups` to true, so set it to false to keep automated backups after deletion.",
